<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bab al-Futuh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bab al-Futuh is one of the monumental gates in the northern wall of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, built in the late eleventh century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a>. Constructed of stone with rounded towers, it is a fine example of Fatimid military architecture. It survives today along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-zuwayla">Bab Zuwayla</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh.jpg" alt="Bab al-Futuh" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo,_porte_settentrionali,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
